NAME
       lvresize - change the size of a logical volume, up or down

SYNOPSIS
       lvresize {-L [+|-]SIZE | -l [+|-]EXTENTS} [-r] [-f] LV [PV...]

DESCRIPTION
       lvresize changes the size of a logical volume in either direction. A
       plus sign grows the LV by that much, a minus sign shrinks it, and a
       size without a sign sets the new total. Growing works the same way as
       lvextend, shrinking the same way as lvreduce.

       With -r the file system is resized together with the LV: grown after
       the LV grows, shrunk before the LV shrinks. XFS file systems can only
       grow; an LV holding XFS cannot be made smaller without losing the
       data.

OPTIONS
       -L, --size [+|-]SIZE
              The new size or the change, with a unit K, M, G or T.

       -l, --extents [+|-]N
              The same in extents, or a percentage: 100%FREE, 50%VG.

       -r, --resizefs
              Resize the file system as well (XFS grow, ext4 grow or shrink).

       -f, --force
              Do not ask before shrinking.

       -y, --yes
              Answer yes to the questions (for example, unmounting an ext4
              file system that must be shrunk).

       -t, --test
              Show what would happen without changing anything.

EXAMPLES
       Grow the LV web and its file system by 1 GiB:

           lvresize -r -L +1G /dev/vgweb/web

       Shrink an ext4 LV to 600 MiB, file system first:

           lvresize -r -L 600M /dev/vgdata/archive

SEE ALSO
       lvextend(8), lvreduce(8), lvs(8), resize2fs(8), xfs_growfs(8), lvm(8)
